-- Update 3: the portfolio rebuild case study and journal post.
-- Run once in Supabase → SQL Editor → New query → paste → Run (after the site with the images is live).
insert into public.posts (slug,title,excerpt,category,published_at,read_minutes,cover,cover_alt,tags,featured,status,blocks)
values ($t$how-i-rebuilt-my-portfolio$t$,$t$I rebuilt my portfolio into a studio. Here’s everything that broke.$t$,$t$From one 766 KB HTML file to a Next.js site with its own admin, price estimates and a 3D gallery. The decisions, the bugs and what I learned.$t$,'code','2026-11-18',7,$t$/assets/rebuild/cover.webp$t$,$t$The 3D gallery hall on my portfolio$t$,
 array[$t$design engineering$t$,$t$next.js$t$,$t$supabase$t$,$t$portfolio$t$,$t$process$t$],true,'published',$j$[["p", "For most of this year my portfolio was one file. A single index.html, 766 KB, holding eleven case studies, a journal and every animation. It looked finished. Under the surface it was fragile: changing one price or fixing one typo meant opening that file and hoping I didn't break something else."], ["p", "In November I decided to turn it into something closer to a product: a site I could edit from an admin, that clients could get a price estimate from, and that still loaded fast on a slow phone in Abuja. This is the honest version of how that went."], ["img", "/assets/rebuild/timeline.webp", "Timeline of the portfolio from July to November 2026", "Five months and nineteen commits, from one HTML file to launch day."], ["h", "Why rebuild something that already worked"], ["p", "I'm starting a studio. A studio portfolio has a different job from a personal one. It has to answer the questions a founder asks before they message you: what would this cost, how long would it take, and how do you work?"], ["p", "My old site couldn't answer any of that without me editing code. So the goal wasn't a new look. I kept the design exactly as it was. The goal was everything behind it."], ["img", "/assets/rebuild/arch.webp", "Diagram: one HTML file on the left, Next.js, Supabase, an admin and a studio funnel on the right", "The design stayed the same. What changed is everything you don’t see."], ["h", "The decisions, and why I made them"], ["l", ["Next.js, so pages are built once and served instantly instead of being assembled on every visit.", "Supabase on its free plan, for posts, case studies, prices, briefs and visit counts, with a small check-in so the free project never goes to sleep.", "My own admin with email and password, because I design admin systems for clients and wanted to live inside one myself.", "No heavy extras on the public pages. If something is big, like the 3D gallery, it only downloads when someone asks for it."]], ["p", "I didn't rewrite eleven case studies by hand. I wrote small scripts that read the old file and turned each section into a component, so the markup and the behaviour stayed identical. Migration scripts are boring, and boring is exactly what you want when you're moving something that already works."], ["h", "What broke (the part nobody posts)"], ["img", "/assets/rebuild/struggles.webp", "Four problems: every page 404, the brief form crashing, a Git question loop, and a password that couldn’t be shown", "Four things that broke on the way to launch."], ["p", "Launch day went wrong straight away. Every page returned 404. The cause was one missing line telling Netlify how to run Next.js. I rolled back to the old site in a minute, added the line, and pushed again."], ["p", "Then the first person to send a brief got “This page couldn’t load”. The live site didn't have the database keys yet, so it tried to save the brief to a file on a server that doesn't allow writing files. Now the keys are in place, and if saving ever fails the visitor gets a friendly message with my email and WhatsApp instead of a crash."], ["p", "Some problems were just unfamiliar. Git on Windows kept asking “Should I try again? (y/n)” forever. I couldn't log in to my own admin because Supabase never shows passwords, by design. Each one felt like a wall for ten minutes and a lesson afterwards."], ["q", "Most bugs weren't hard. They were invisible until the moment a real person used the thing."], ["h", "The studio part: an estimate in two minutes"], ["p", "The new Services page lets someone pick what they need, how big it is and how their team works. A timeline and a price range update as they go, in naira or dollars. When they send the brief, it's saved in my admin, emailed to me and they can carry on the conversation on WhatsApp."], ["img", "/assets/rebuild/estimate.webp", "The Services page estimate changing as options are picked", "Every choice updates the timeline and price range."], ["h", "Story mode: six tries to get it right"], ["p", "I wanted visitors to feel my case studies, not just scroll past them. The first version was a scrolling story. Then a tap-through one. Then a cinematic one. Then a curved gallery room, inspired by an art site I loved. It still felt flat, so I built a full 3D hall you walk through in first person, one exhibit per case study, each with a short write-up and a Play story button."], ["img", "/assets/rebuild/story-evolution.webp", "Four versions of story mode side by side", "From a flat gallery to a 3D hall. Every version taught me what the next one needed."], ["img", "/assets/rebuild/hall-walk.webp", "Walking through the 3D hall from one case study to the next", "Walking the hall. It only downloads when someone switches Story mode on, and weak phones get a lighter version."], ["h", "An admin I'd hand to a client"], ["p", "Everything is editable now: journal posts, case study text and covers, prices, homepage text and links. Briefs arrive with one-tap replies in Gmail or WhatsApp. There's a Visits page showing where people come from and which case studies they open, without cookies or storing anyone's IP address."], ["img", "/assets/rebuild/admin-case-edit.webp", "The case study editor in the admin", "Editing a case study. Empty fields keep the designed text."], ["h", "What I'd tell a founder (or a recruiter)"], ["img", "/assets/rebuild/lessons.webp", "Four steps: ask, prototype, test on a phone, ship once", "The loop I followed every day."], ["l", ["I ask before I assume. One question with three options saves a day of rework.", "I prototype where nothing is at risk. Every Story mode version was tested on a private page before it touched the live site.", "I test on a phone, on a normal network. If it's slow there, it isn't done.", "I ship in one batch and check the live result myself. Hosting credits are real money, so I don't push every small change."]], ["p", "I'm a product designer learning to build what I design. This rebuild was the biggest step so far, and I documented every lesson in the code so I understand why each line is there."], ["cta", "Want something built like this, or want me on your team?", "/services", "Get an estimate →", "", "blue"]]$j$::jsonb)
on conflict (slug) do update set title=excluded.title,excerpt=excluded.excerpt,blocks=excluded.blocks,cover=excluded.cover,cover_alt=excluded.cover_alt,tags=excluded.tags,featured=true,status='published',updated_at=now();
update public.posts set featured=false where slug<>$t$how-i-rebuilt-my-portfolio$t$ and featured;

insert into public.case_studies (slug,title,kind,sort_order,status,card,brief,sections)
values ($t$portfolio-rebuild$t$,$t$Rebuilding my portfolio into a studio$t$,$t$Design engineering · Next.js, Supabase$t$,0,'published',$j${"custom": true, "cover": "/assets/rebuild/cover.webp", "intro": "My portfolio was one 766 KB HTML file. I rebuilt it into a fast Next.js site with its own admin, instant price estimates for clients, private visit stats and a 3D gallery, while keeping the design exactly as it was.", "desc": "How a product designer rebuilt his portfolio into a studio site: Next.js, Supabase, a custom admin, a pricing estimator and a 3D story mode.", "flow": [{"img": "/assets/rebuild/new-home.webp", "t": "Homepage", "b": "Same design as before, now built once and cached so it opens fast."}, {"img": "/assets/rebuild/estimate.webp", "t": "Get an estimate", "b": "Pick the project, size and team. Timeline and price update live."}, {"img": "/assets/rebuild/hall-walk.webp", "t": "Story mode", "b": "A first-person 3D hall with one exhibit per case study."}, {"img": "/assets/rebuild/story-chapter.webp", "t": "Play story", "b": "Each case study told in short cinematic chapters."}, {"img": "/assets/rebuild/admin-overview.webp", "t": "Admin overview", "b": "Visits, new briefs and posts at a glance."}, {"img": "/assets/rebuild/admin-case-edit.webp", "t": "Case study editor", "b": "Edit text, cover and the design flow without code."}, {"img": "/assets/rebuild/admin-links.webp", "t": "Homepage & links", "b": "Change the intro and every contact link across the site."}]}$j$::jsonb,$j${"problem": "Every change to my portfolio meant editing one huge file, and the site couldn’t answer the first things a client asks: what will it cost, how long, and how do you work.", "did": "I designed and built the whole thing: ported eleven case studies with migration scripts, set up Supabase, designed and coded the admin, the Services estimator, brief emails, visit stats and Story mode.", "result": "The same design, now fully editable from my own admin. Clients get a timeline and price range in two minutes, every brief reaches my inbox and WhatsApp, and each case study can be walked through in 3D.", "trade": "The 3D gallery is the most impressive part and the heaviest. It only downloads when someone switches Story mode on, and weak phones get a lighter version, so the rest of the site stays fast.", "next": "Move more of the homepage into the admin, connect my content tool so new case studies become social posts, and keep measuring which stories bring in briefs."}$j$::jsonb,$j$[{"h": "The problem", "p": "The old site looked finished, but it was one 766 KB file. Fixing a typo or changing a price meant editing code and hoping nothing else broke.\n\nI’m starting a studio, and a studio site has a different job. It has to answer what a project costs, how long it takes and how I work, before anyone messages me.", "img": "/assets/rebuild/arch.webp", "cap": "The design stayed. Everything behind it changed."}, {"h": "How it went", "p": "Five months, nineteen commits. The rebuild itself took a few intense days in November, mostly spent on things that went wrong at launch.", "img": "/assets/rebuild/timeline.webp"}, {"h": "Choices I made", "p": "Next.js so pages are built once and served instantly. Supabase on the free plan for everything that changes, with a small scheduled check-in so it never sleeps. My own admin, because I design admin systems for clients and wanted to use one daily.\n\nI didn’t rewrite the case studies by hand. Small scripts converted the old file section by section, so the layout and behaviour stayed identical."}, {"h": "What broke", "p": "Every page returned 404 on launch: one missing setting. The brief form crashed: the live site didn’t have its database keys. Git kept asking the same question forever, and I locked myself out of my own admin.\n\nEach one took minutes to fix once I understood it. Now the form fails politely, every fix is written down, and changes go out in one batch so hosting credits aren’t wasted.", "img": "/assets/rebuild/struggles.webp"}, {"h": "The studio funnel", "p": "Visitors pick what they need and see a timeline and a price range in naira or dollars. Sending a brief saves it in my admin, emails me with one-tap replies, and opens WhatsApp to keep talking.", "img": "/assets/rebuild/estimate.webp", "cap": "The estimate updates with every choice."}, {"h": "Story mode", "p": "Six versions in two days: a scrolling story, tap-through, cinematic, a flat gallery, a curved room and finally a first-person 3D hall. I tested each one privately before choosing, so none of the experiments cost anything live.", "img": "/assets/rebuild/story-evolution.webp"}, {"h": "How I work", "p": "Ask instead of assuming. Prototype where nothing is at risk. Test on a real phone on a normal network. Ship once and check the live site myself.", "img": "/assets/rebuild/lessons.webp"}]$j$::jsonb)
on conflict (slug) do update set title=excluded.title,kind=excluded.kind,card=excluded.card,brief=excluded.brief,sections=excluded.sections,status='published',updated_at=now();
